#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198084 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "efdb77bf-dd8b-5ccc-8265-79bd319afaa3" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Inequalities"}] :question/id #uuid "1a2f8100-8f2e-5c6b-ade3-7192a914323f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13171" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A = \\{n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: : \\: n^2-9 \\geq 0 \\}$\n\n- $B = \\{m \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: : \\: m^2-9 \\leq 0 \\}$\n\n- $C = \\{q \\in \\mathbb{Q} \\: : \\: q^2-9 \\leq 0 \\}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y=x^2-9$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-3$ and $x=3,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/assets/3c2fc2f1eeb1d4e92f7933a408dafe7c3a216aa3d899f4025c71407140d47de1.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality $x^2-9 \\geq 0$ for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is \n\n$$\nx \\leq -3 \\quad\\text{or}\\quad x \\geq 3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x \\in I_1 = \\left(-\\infty, -3\\right] \\cup \\left[3,\\infty\\right).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality $x^2-9 \\leq 0$ for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is \n\n$$\n-3 \\leq x \\leq 3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x \\in I_2 = [-3,3].$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ the solutions to the inequality $n^2-9 \\geq 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I_1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA = \\{ \\ldots, -4, -3 \\} \\cup \\{ 3, 4, \\ldots \\}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. If $m \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ the solutions to the inequality $m^2-9 \\leq 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I_2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB = \\{0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3\\}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B| = 7,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q \\in \\mathbb{Q},$ the solutions to $q^2-9 \\leq 0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I_2.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198085 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f98332fd-e902-5390-a4d3-4e181ce97219" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Trigonometric Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "3ccf363e-d311-50e9-83b1-929ef1148554" :question/math-academy-id "e-13174" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A = \\{n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: : \\: \\sin (n\\pi) = 1 \\}$\n\n- $B = \\{q \\in \\mathbb{Q} \\: : \\: \\sin (q\\pi) = 1 \\}$\n\n- $C = \\{x \\in \\mathbb{R} \\: : \\: \\sin (x\\pi) = 1 \\}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation $\\sin (x\\pi) = 1$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\pi  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2m\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}+2m,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There are no values of $m\\in\\mathbb Z$ such that $\\dfrac{1}{2} + 2m \\in\\mathbb Z.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA = \\{\\}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A| = 0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nB = \\left\\{\\ldots,-\\dfrac{3}{2},\\dfrac12, \\dfrac{5}{2}, \\dfrac{9}{2},\\ldots\\right\\}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B \\subseteq C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198086 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26758016-ed47-562a-9338-85c71ffbeaa9" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Derivatives"}] :question/id #uuid "359028be-0c1d-5f5c-ace7-3d7aeb36d9e6" :question/math-academy-id "e-13175" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A = \\left\\{f^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(e^{5x})  \\: : \\:  n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$\n\n- $B = \\left\\{g^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(6e^{-x})  \\: : \\:  n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$\n\n- $C =  \\left\\{h^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(2e^{x+3})  \\: : \\:  n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{5x} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{5x})=5{e}^{5x} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{e}^{5x})=25{e}^{5x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(25{e}^{5x})=125{e}^{5x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nA = \\{e^{5x}, 5e^{5x}, 25e^{5x}, 125e^{5x}, \\ldots\\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =6{e}^{-x} \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{e}^{-x})=-6{e}^{-x} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-6{e}^{-x})=6{e}^{-x} \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{e}^{-x})=-6{e}^{-x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $B$ contains only two elements:\n\n$$\nB = \\{6e^{-x}, -6e^{-x}\\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B| = 2,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is also finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =2{e}^{x+3} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x+3})=2{e}^{x+3} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x+3})=2{e}^{x+3} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x+3})=2{e}^{x+3} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $C$ contains only one element:\n\n$$\nC = \\{ 2e^{x+3} \\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|C| = 1,$ which is finite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "efdb77bf-dd8b-5ccc-8265-79bd319afaa3" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb3bbee5-0ea0-57e9-9979-9fb6497efbf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195266} {:answer/id #uuid "d585b964-7781-5f66-b454-f1b872b94f98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195267} {:answer/id #uuid "22d011d0-78d4-5a16-a572-72ba36a62857" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195268} {:answer/id #uuid "26cb01aa-c87a-59f2-bc07-ef883cadda07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195269} {:answer/id #uuid "596f09fb-51fa-5bd0-8c1e-8a80797a93bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22d011d0-78d4-5a16-a572-72ba36a62857" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195268} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca482694-e753-5b94-a4be-f0e5e63f7310" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9482778-6f45-5e93-913d-289acb526444" :question/math-academy-id "q-181536" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,{n}^{2}-5n+6>0}$\n- $B={m\\in Z\\,:\\,{m}^{2}-5m+6<0}$\n- $C={q\\in Q\\,:\\,{q}^{2}-5q+6<0}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y={x}^{2}-5x+6$ for $x\\in R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5x+6 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=2$ and $x=3,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/assets/0a42815f626652c332862a38a3812463a9407605329bd81df25f11274b26ce6f.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-5x+6>0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\nx<2\\,\\text{or}\\,x>3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{1}=(-\\infty ,2)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-5x+6<0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\n2<x<3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{2}=(2,3).$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${n}^{2}-5n+6>0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={…,0,1}\\cup {4,5,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. If $m\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${m}^{2}-5m+6<0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to ${q}^{2}-5q+6<0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f98332fd-e902-5390-a4d3-4e181ce97219" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Trigonometric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d8977de-4d1b-5a0f-abf2-f8970d0b056f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195278} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc5d032-c92d-5d49-891c-26a5377a22e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195279} {:answer/id #uuid "0a32bb9d-dc47-5a86-97c7-78c20c47e5c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195280} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea2f1f4-7e3e-5782-997a-e1e3de251b54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195281} {:answer/id #uuid "71e74963-91d2-5eef-b367-b092a3424962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d8977de-4d1b-5a0f-abf2-f8970d0b056f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195278} :answer-field/id #uuid "45de3bee-dbad-5b10-affc-2a7a874ef8cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4e83b937-f1ab-5064-ab2b-f81fab22743d" :question/math-academy-id "q-181597" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2n\\pi )=1}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2q\\pi )=1}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\pi )=1}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\pi )=1$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\pi  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2m\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4}+m,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There are no values of $m\\in Z$ such that $\\frac{1}{4}+m\\in Z.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nB={…,-\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{9}{4},…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊆C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198714 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26758016-ed47-562a-9338-85c71ffbeaa9" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d43098da-8501-52f2-8ecd-616271ac75cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195284} {:answer/id #uuid "e3cb8316-aecd-5b74-a0b8-3e719fa59171" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195285} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd0734b-ee1c-57ce-b7f2-dacf18348b4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195286} {:answer/id #uuid "43f10ba1-151c-591b-9c0b-3f2a77cb1565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195287} {:answer/id #uuid "5620289a-49c2-5c6b-b554-006fc956cb94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d43098da-8501-52f2-8ecd-616271ac75cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195284} :answer-field/id #uuid "78def06e-bdb9-5f5d-8322-394a11bbb4cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e02e6bd4-a148-57c6-a612-60228a140b9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-181599" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({x}^{2})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\sqrt{x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\n- The set $A$ is finite. Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})=2x \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)=2 \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2)=0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(0)=0 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, for $n>3,$ we have ${f}^{(n)}(x)=0.$ Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\nA={{x}^{2},2x,2,0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=4,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB={\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\\frac{1}{x},-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Similar to the previous case, listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})=-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}})=\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC={\\sqrt{x},\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}},-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}},\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306567 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26758016-ed47-562a-9338-85c71ffbeaa9" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "580e193f-ca43-544a-9231-ad81e85c1481" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306569} {:answer/id #uuid "b33ec1e3-87b6-5c4a-ad7f-f1d755e2dff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306570} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f5b712-ab68-5c77-826b-82db343f304d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306571} {:answer/id #uuid "bffc7639-a9b5-501d-8e81-f0e153f0edf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306572} {:answer/id #uuid "5d9ca6dc-b0c5-518b-8a65-6f98b54820ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b33ec1e3-87b6-5c4a-ad7f-f1d755e2dff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306570} :answer-field/id #uuid "678a0839-4d7f-56bc-a627-09ad40e3e36f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85aa147c-d45d-5ffd-8f95-23ef4425b791" :question/math-academy-id "q-181643" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{x-1})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{-2x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{x/2})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{x-1} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x-1})={e}^{x-1} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x-1})={e}^{x-1} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x-1})={e}^{x-1} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $A$ contains only one element:\n\n$$\nA={{e}^{x-1}}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{-2x} \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{-2x})=-2{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{e}^{-2x})=4{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{e}^{-2x})=-8{e}^{-2x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB={{e}^{-2x},-2{e}^{-2x},4{e}^{-2x},-8{e}^{-2x},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{x/2} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x/2})=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x/2} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x/2})=\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x/2} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x/2})=\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{x/2} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC={{e}^{x/2},\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x/2},\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x/2},\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{x/2},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198719 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f98332fd-e902-5390-a4d3-4e181ce97219" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Trigonometric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abbc3e10-b210-5fbd-91b6-cf0444936e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195319} {:answer/id #uuid "af481dcc-2df0-5c95-b1dd-0c1fa1f79f71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the sets are infinite." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195320} {:answer/id #uuid "bd50ec20-84b0-57ae-9a4e-2f1f734d9dbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195321} {:answer/id #uuid "dd6975a5-8cd7-5d25-b251-4c3ec31a2d0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A, B$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195322} {:answer/id #uuid "71bac5b8-cff3-5995-8451-3a97664bf3a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71bac5b8-cff3-5995-8451-3a97664bf3a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195323} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1af7eea-31a3-54f3-a457-f175984dd3f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d5b151a-6cd5-5110-8e22-9ecd5205fa7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-181668" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡n=0}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡q=0}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0}$\n\nHint: The arguments of the trigonometric functions are in radians." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nWe start by writing down the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+m\\pi ,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There are no values of $m\\in Z$ such that $\\frac{\\pi }{2}+m\\pi \\in Z.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Similar to the above, there are no values of $m\\in Q$ such that $\\frac{\\pi }{2}+m\\pi \\in Q.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $C$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nC={…,-\\frac{3\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2},…}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306595 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f98332fd-e902-5390-a4d3-4e181ce97219" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Trigonometric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f2c571e-77ae-53f1-a7f0-1fe5df7df528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306597} {:answer/id #uuid "4436908e-15b5-5856-9282-80bcb213d393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306598} {:answer/id #uuid "338deccf-c807-561b-900a-758dad384517" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306599} {:answer/id #uuid "30ccc693-5a91-59f5-bb1a-45ed750e2293" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306600} {:answer/id #uuid "a493e4a3-8697-53c5-9ed1-a611d01441ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a493e4a3-8697-53c5-9ed1-a611d01441ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306601} :answer-field/id #uuid "29f60c14-1b5f-510a-a4fe-c726856ecefa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "348023c8-2c6e-59e8-ab9b-8e7c4f11d331" :question/math-academy-id "q-181674" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2})=0}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{q\\pi }{2})=0}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{2})=0}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{2})=0$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x\\pi }{2} & =m\\pi  \\\\ x & =2m,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite.Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nA={…,-2,0,2,4,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Since $A$ is infinite, and $A⊆B,$ it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊆C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306622 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "efdb77bf-dd8b-5ccc-8265-79bd319afaa3" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f540d802-d458-5ac6-8630-46ef1a25e7bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306624} {:answer/id #uuid "80be8dd9-6b59-59b7-9e3d-4d175fbf640e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306625} {:answer/id #uuid "8582fbef-27fe-558b-8723-b35cf858666b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306626} {:answer/id #uuid "0e83f66d-ebeb-514e-8584-d2ebc13f33cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306627} {:answer/id #uuid "4c79b3d3-e1ea-56fe-8a99-5732d5047ece" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8582fbef-27fe-558b-8723-b35cf858666b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306626} :answer-field/id #uuid "700b2c4c-c3f9-5c4f-8012-1b2f252111d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "031a254f-645d-5e04-8e60-c9c3f41f5866" :question/math-academy-id "q-181740" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,-{n}^{2}+1>0}$\n- $B={m\\in Z\\,:\\,-{m}^{2}+1<0}$\n- $C={q\\in Q\\,:\\,-{q}^{2}+1<0}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y=-{x}^{2}+1$ for $x\\in R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ -(x-1)(x+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-1$ and $x=1,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/assets/0699524ff20f871c9eeca93faa9934832b8e5816fd23b46862e8b01d48e7d86b.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality $-{x}^{2}+1>0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\n-1<x<1\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{1}=(-1,1).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality $-{x}^{2}+1<0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\nx<-1\\,\\text{or}\\,x>1\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{2}=(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $-{n}^{2}+1>0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $m\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $-{m}^{2}+1<0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={…,-3,-2}\\cup {2,3,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $-{q}^{2}+1<0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306629 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "efdb77bf-dd8b-5ccc-8265-79bd319afaa3" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ff48d5b-8abe-50e9-be1b-b1e83d5bfc34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306631} {:answer/id #uuid "78fecbec-a69f-5fbc-91c5-dcd4ef42b4ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306632} {:answer/id #uuid "c51e53ab-eaf5-55c3-95bb-592cb8c1a4fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306633} {:answer/id #uuid "d97dd7bc-2165-5e7a-b5c3-0451a7968a55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306634} {:answer/id #uuid "733f25df-445a-5226-a1d8-b337731f3665" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78fecbec-a69f-5fbc-91c5-dcd4ef42b4ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306632} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f567e81-ce4d-5601-9660-f914e526cbba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b59438ef-9a39-5774-b8fc-c511ad8094bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-181768" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,{n}^{2}-2n\\le 0}$\n- $B={m\\in Z\\,:\\,{m}^{2}-2m\\ge 0}$\n- $C={q\\in Q\\,:\\,{q}^{2}-2q\\le 0}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y={x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=0$ and $x=2,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/assets/496c6d53993ca6cae499f617bca42dbc42e4c57bffa278da6312a5b1723bdcbc.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-2x\\le 0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{1}=[0,2].$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-2x\\ge 0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\nx\\le 0\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge 2\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{2}=(-\\infty ,0]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${n}^{2}-2n\\le 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={0,1,2}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=3,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $m\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${m}^{2}-2m\\ge 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={…,-1,0}\\cup {2,3,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to ${q}^{2}-2q\\le 0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\""}]}
